.DS_Store
Konjugieren.xcodeproj/project.xcworkspace/xcuserdata/
Konjugieren.xcodeproj/xcuserdata/
.claude/settings.local.json
Secrets.xcconfig
# The corpus texts are licensed sources and its JSON is a regenerable build product, so both stay
# ignored — but not the pipeline scripts that live beside them. Without the negations below,
# `corpus/working/build_corpus_index.py` is untracked and a fresh clone loses the German-specific
# rules encoded in it (Satzklammer particle matching, two-column PDF recovery, excluding the
# English translations) with no way to recover them. Git does not descend into an excluded
# directory, so each path component has to be re-included before the final rule can match.
corpus/*
!corpus/working/
corpus/working/*
!corpus/working/*.py
# Same reasoning as the scripts: the mining spec is knowledge a fresh clone would
# otherwise lose, and Phase 4 spans more than one session, so the subagents' brief
# has to live in git rather than in a scratchpad.
!corpus/working/*.md
# Mined shard OUTPUT is authored scholarship and is not regenerable: an etymology composed
# from the reuse files plus a sentence chosen and translated by hand. Shard INPUT is a pure
# build product, rebuilt by `build_mining_shards.py` from `forms.json` and the reuse files,
# so it stays ignored. Until 2026-07-21 there was no rule here at all and every session had
# to `git add -f` its own outputs; 27 shards survived because someone remembered each time,
# and six from that day's session were sitting untracked when it was noticed. Since the
# resume protocol derives remaining work from which `.out.json` exist, a forgotten add does
# not merely lose the scholarship — it silently reports the shard as unmined.
!corpus/working/shards/
corpus/working/shards/*
!corpus/working/shards/*.out.json
# ios-build-verify
build.log
.claude/ios-build-verify.*
docs/screenshots/
# WWDC transcript (Apple copyrighted; archived locally for reference only)
docs/wwdc2026-platforms-sotu-transcript.txt
docs/wwdc2026-whats-new-in-swift-transcript.txt
docs/wwdc2026-whats-new-swiftui-transcript.txt
# kaikki.org Wiktionary extraction (~294 MB; re-download recipe in verbdata/README.md)
verbdata/*.jsonl
# DWDS frequency snapshot: BBAW reserves § 44b UrhG and bars redistribution
# without permission. Untracked pending a reply from dwds@bbaw.de.
verbdata/dwds-frequencies.json
# Classify-and-verify pipeline outputs: large, derived, and regenerable in ~45
# seconds from the scripts beside them. See docs/verb-classification.md.
verbdata/candidates.json
verbdata/classification.json
# Written by build_candidates.py --all-records for the dual-auxiliary pass; a superset of
# candidates.json that keeps every Wiktionary record per lemma, so it is just as large.
verbdata/readings.json
verbdata/classification-summary.md

# Python bytecode from the verbdata/ scripts
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Authoring-run raw child telemetry: 2.4 MB of harness boilerplate whose useful fields
# (thinking_tokens, num_turns) are folded into metrics.jsonl. See prompts/example_analysis.md.
verbdata/authored/meta/
verbdata/authored/run.log

# Review INPUT shards: 1.1 MB fully derived from the authored shards, candidates.json, and
# forms.json. Regenerate with verbdata/authored/build_review_shards.py. The .out.json findings
# beside them are model output and are NOT derivable, so they stay tracked.
verbdata/review/shards/*.in.json
# Reviewer raw child telemetry, as with the authoring run.
verbdata/review/meta/
verbdata/review/run.log
